---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TableTour from '../components/TableTour.astro';
import { QTABLE_UI_GITHUB_URL, withBase } from '../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout title="QTableUI — React frontend for QTable | QingZoneX" description="Explore QTableUI: React 19 product UI for work centers, five table views, dashboards, automation, collaboration, AI and self-hosted QTable.">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="shell"><span class="eyebrow"><i class="eyebrow-dot"></i> QTableUI · v0.1.0-alpha</span><h1>The application layer<br/>for the QTable work model.</h1><p>QTableUI is not only a grid renderer. The current baseline includes Home/My Work, Projects, Dashboard and Automation centers, five table views, notifications, record collaboration, AI workflows, Source Inbox and recovery surfaces.</p><div class="page-actions"><a class="button primary" href={withBase('docs/qtable-ui/overview/')}>Read QTableUI docs →</a><a class="button secondary" href={QTABLE_UI_GITHUB_URL}>Source on GitHub</a></div><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>React 19</strong><span>application foundation</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>TypeScript 6</strong><span>typed product codebase</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>VTable</strong><span>Grid + Gantt rendering</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>Apollo + Zustand</strong><span>server and interaction state</span></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><span class="kicker">Product surfaces</span><h2>A real work shell around the table model.</h2><p>The OSS release closure removed user-visible planned placeholders from primary navigation and connected existing backend capabilities to real product surfaces.</p></div><div class="feature-grid">
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">⌂</div><h3>Home / My Work</h3><p>Server-backed work aggregation helps users see what needs attention before opening a specific table.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">P</div><h3>Projects Center</h3><p>Task Profile/My Work-backed project summaries, progress/risk metrics and deep links reuse existing work semantics.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">◇</div><h3>Dashboard Center</h3><p>Workspace dashboards, workbench editing, charts, filters, sharing and permission-aware management.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">⚡</div><h3>Automation Center</h3><p>Real automation list, rules, validation, enable/disable, test/run and execution history.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">✉</div><h3>Notifications</h3><p>Realtime notification bell/center and deep-link paths connect assignments, collaboration and activity.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">▣</div><h3>Record Workspace</h3><p>Record detail/collaboration surfaces bring fields, source context and activity into the current view context.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">⌕</div><h3>Global discovery</h3><p>Command/search paths help recover tables and records while respecting backend visibility.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">↺</div><h3>Recycle Bin</h3><p>Delete safety supports restore and explicit purge semantics instead of implying recovery through a placeholder.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">✦</div><h3>AI & Source Inbox</h3><p>AI planning, Project Steward and QNote/Clipper intake expose existing QTable AI/source contracts in the product.</p></article>
  </div></div></section>

  <section class="section alt"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><span class="kicker">Five views</span><h2>Choose the operating lens without changing the data model.</h2><p>This portal tour is simulated locally; QTableUI itself connects to QTable and inherits its permission model.</p></div><TableTour compact /></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><span class="kicker">Frontend stack</span><h2>Modern application primitives, product-specific contracts.</h2></div><div class="feature-grid">
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">R</div><h3>React 19</h3><p>Application shell and interactive product surfaces.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">TS</div><h3>TypeScript 6</h3><p>Typed logic across views, workflows and integrations.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">VT</div><h3>VTable / Gantt</h3><p>High-density grid and Gantt rendering.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">A</div><h3>Apollo Client</h3><p>GraphQL data layer aligned to backend contracts.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">Z</div><h3>Zustand</h3><p>Focused client interaction state.</p></article>
    <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">VC</div><h3>VChart + grid layout</h3><p>Dashboard visualization and flexible composition.</p></article>
  </div></div></section>

  <section class="section alt"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><span class="kicker">Runtime & distribution</span><h2>Designed to run beside a self-hosted QTable API.</h2><p>The production image uses Nginx and proxies REST/API, GraphQL, WebSocket, Auth and OAuth traffic to QTable. A container health endpoint is available at <code>/healthz</code>.</p></div><div class="two-col"><article class="product-card"><h3>Container runtime</h3><ul><li>PORT defaults to 9100</li><li>QTABLE_HOST / QTABLE_PORT select the backend</li><li>SPA deep-link and proxy smoke tests in CI</li><li>Official image/registry defaults with explicit mirror overrides</li></ul></article><article class="product-card ui"><h3>Deployment options</h3><ul><li>Canonical full stack from QTable Docker Compose</li><li>Standalone QTableUI container</li><li>Rainbond configuration remains supported</li><li>SBOM, dependency audit and license-policy artifacts in CI</li></ul></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><span class="kicker">Product invariants</span><h2>The frontend does not weaken backend safety.</h2><p>Client features are expected to preserve permission, paging and audited mutation contracts.</p></div><div class="two-col"><div class="product-card"><h3>Preserve</h3><ul><li>Server paging and aggregation on large-table paths</li><li>Workspace-scoped member candidates</li><li>Public-token-safe dashboard APIs</li><li>Preview → Confirm → Apply for AI writes</li><li>Backend-acknowledged mutation results</li></ul></div><div class="product-card ui"><h3>Avoid</h3><ul><li>Loading hidden rows for client AI/analytics</li><li>Direct writes when atomic audited mutations exist</li><li>Private business data in generic offline caches</li><li>Real credentials in frontend environment variables</li></ul></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="cta"><div class="shell"><div class="cta-card"><h2>Develop QTableUI against a local QTable API.</h2><p>Node.js 22 and npm/package-lock are the supported reproducible frontend path. Review the feature matrix for current Alpha surfaces and active hardening.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href={withBase('docs/qtable-ui/development/')}>Development guide →</a><a class="button secondary" href={withBase('docs/project/feature-matrix/')}>Feature matrix</a><a class="button ghost" href={QTABLE_UI_GITHUB_URL}>Inspect source</a></div></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
